{{ deployNav("overview", deploy.pages) }}

<section class="almanac-section deploy-options">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="almanac-section-heading">
      <span class="almanac-section-index">01—04</span>
      <div><p class="almanac-kicker">Choose an install path</p><h2>Same boundary. Different operating shape.</h2></div>
      <p>The current application ships through native package managers, attested archives, and a multi-architecture image. The published Helm chart moves independently and currently trails the application.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="deploy-option-grid">
      <article class="deploy-option-card">
        <div><span>01</span>{{ status("current", "macOS + Linux") }}</div>
        <h2>Homebrew</h2>
        <p>Install all four executables and the broker-hosted JSONPlaceholder example from the organization tap.</p>
        <pre><code>brew install dekopon-agents/tap/dekopon</code></pre>
        <small>The generated formula is pinned to the current attested release archives.</small>
      </article>

      <article class="deploy-option-card">
        <div><span>02</span>{{ status("current", "pinned Rust install") }}</div>
        <h2>crates.io</h2>
        <p>Install an exact release of any public Dekopon crate with Cargo. The current release publishes all 25 public workspace crates.</p>
        <pre><code>cargo install --locked \
  --version {{ release.version }} dekopon-run</code></pre>
        <small>Attested archives remain available for macOS arm64 and Linux arm64/amd64 on the GitHub release.</small>
      </article>

      <article class="deploy-option-card">
        <div><span>03</span>{{ status("current", "multi-architecture") }}</div>
        <h2>Container image</h2>
        <p>Run the exact Linux executable bytes from the release archives in one distroless image with broker provider components.</p>
        <pre><code>docker pull \
  {{ release.imageReference }}</code></pre>
        <small>Runs as numeric UID 65532. No shell, config, policy, credential, or audit state is baked in.</small>
      </article>

      <article class="deploy-option-card deploy-option-featured">
        <div><span>04</span>{{ status("direction", "published chart trails app") }}</div>
        <h2>Kubernetes</h2>
        <p>Published chart {{ release.chart.publishedVersion }} deploys application {{ release.chart.publishedAppVersion }}. Chart {{ release.chart.sourceVersion }} for application {{ release.chart.sourceAppVersion }} exists in source but is not yet published.</p>
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/deploy/kubernetes/">Understand the pod <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <small>Registry: <code>oci://ghcr.io/dekopon-agents/charts/dekopon</code>. A source version is not a published install path.</small>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="almanac-section deploy-invariants">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="almanac-section-heading">
      <span class="almanac-section-index">∞</span>
      <div><p class="almanac-kicker">What never changes</p><h2>Packaging does not grant authority.</h2></div>
      <p>Every deployment supplies its own identities, Cedar policy, constraint sets, credentials, and state.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="invariant-notes">
      <article><span>gateway</span><h3>Hears and reasons</h3><p>Chat and model credentials terminate here. Provider credentials and policy do not.</p></article>
      <article><span>broker</span><h3>Decides and executes</h3><p>Trusted identity, Cedar, execution constraints, provider credentials, evidence, and audit stay here.</p></article>
      <article><span>provider</span><h3>Performs one operation</h3><p>A fresh Wasm instance gets only explicitly linked, authorization-bound host services.</p></article>
      <article><span>operator</span><h3>Owns the files</h3><p>The deployment must provide safe configuration, credential, socket, and audit paths.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="almanac-closing-band deploy-closing-band">
  <div class="shell">
    <span>The most constrained deployment is also the most interesting</span>
